From 7a1a32c36e3ec01f97b10ad79c28e418dfcec96f Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Ian Blenke <ian@blenke.com>
Date: Sun, 12 Jul 2026 00:16:14 -0400
Subject: [PATCH 06843/11362] ARC round-2 (codex): s5i5 honest null on
 relaunch, L4 mechanism sharpened

Final game of the round-2 breadth campaign, on a relaunch (45-minute
budget) after the first attempt hit the 1800s process timeout. Unlike
lf52/re86, this fresh session did NOT replicate the win the first
attempt's (unpersisted) transcript had claimed -- an honest null, not a
fabrication: per this project's discipline, a claim with no persisted
artifact does not count regardless of what an earlier transcript showed.

Real progress on understanding, though: this session found a sharper
mechanism than the prior null (each marker-bar grow raises the D marker
one band AND simultaneously widens all four cantilever shelves, making
shelf ORDER load-bearing, not just position) and a specific, actionable
hypothesis the prior attempt lacked (the two inner shelves likely must
exchange vertical order early, around grow 4, before their widths
overlap and the order becomes irreversible at grow 8).

Also ran a regression check on the existing banked L3 prefix (unrelated
to this session's null) after the agent flagged a shortened variant that
failed to reproduce -- confirmed the actual banked prefix still replays
cleanly, 3x fresh env.reset(), no drift.

No new level banked; reproducible_total_levels stays 138. This closes out
all 18 round-2 games: 3 full clears (vc33, cn04, lp85's terminal L8), 8
level-ups (sp80, m0r0, ka59, tu93, su15, r11l, re86, lf52), and 7 honest
documented nulls (bp35, g50t, dc22, sk48, sc25, ls20, s5i5) -- each of
which meaningfully narrowed the search for a future attempt rather than
being a dead loss.

